WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.625
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Have you ever wanted

00:00:01.625 --> 00:00:03.330
to know how health
care companies deal

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:06.330
with large amount of data
coming from multiple different

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:07.230
sources?

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.120
Well, I've been
really curious how

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.030
our insurance claims, lab
reports, and other data

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:13.470
is put to use.

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:16.830
So today, I invited Amol
Kher, VP of engineering

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.740
from Vida Health, a really
cool health care startup

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.590
creating a personalized
experience for patients.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:25.510
Welcome, Amol, and thanks
for joining us today.

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:28.598
AMOL KHER: Thanks, Priyanka,
looking forward to this chat.

00:00:28.598 --> 00:00:31.140
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Awesome So
tell us more about Vida Health.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.723
AMOL KHER: Yeah, so
if I also describe

00:00:32.723 --> 00:00:35.010
Vida in one sentence
or one line,

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.500
it would be that we are an
additional health, virtual care

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:38.040
company.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.250
And we connect both
mind and body programs

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.230
into a single platform.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.360
A lot of companies
that you see out

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.300
are often treating
patients for one condition.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.240
And our platform
actually supports

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:51.490
multiple different conditions.

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:54.780
So it really helps
people when they

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.330
can go to this one
single place and get

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.435
different kinds of treatment
on a single platform.

00:00:59.435 --> 00:01:00.810
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, awesome.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.852
So it sounds like there
are three different types

00:01:02.852 --> 00:01:03.510
of customers.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.100
There are the individuals like
us, who may need the help.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.340
And then there are health
plans, and then you

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.740
have the employers,
who are helping.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.440
So let's double click
on that a little bit.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.250
What are the challenges
you actually set out

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.720
to solve for these
three customers?

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.050
AMOL KHER: So as
you can see, we have

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.690
a lot of different kinds
of data that's coming in.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.550
And we have to connect
all of that stuff

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.290
together in a seamless manner.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.990
And we have to host
this, and we have

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.680
services that are
mobile focused,

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.850
but there are services that
are for web on the backend.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.070
And so it's a pretty
interesting challenge.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.070
And I'm glad that
we've consolidated

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.160
everything on Google now.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.460
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Awesome, so ease of use

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.270
is definitely one that you're
pointing out as the reason

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:49.590
to choose Google Cloud.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:50.940
Are there any others?

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.740
AMOL KHER: Yeah,
as I looked at--

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.900
when we were evaluating
Google Cloud,

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.760
really, there were
three big areas

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:02.910
that came to me as reasons why
consolidating in GCP was great.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:07.230
One was just the cost reduction,
because after consolidating

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.690
everything, it's just
one single place where

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.130
you can drive efficiency.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.410
And we've already seen
somewhere around, like,

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:18.150
50% reduction in cost just
by bringing everything

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.440
in one place.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.165
You can optimize, fine
tune different things.

00:02:22.165 --> 00:02:23.790
The other thing I
would say it was more

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.600
around developer productivity,
because now engineers

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.850
don't have to worry
about where to deploy,

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.390
and, kind of, keep that
cognitive overload much

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.420
more lower because it's one
single place that they know

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.400
how to look at and monitor.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:39.570
So they are happy.

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:41.820
And if they are happy, they're
more productive, right?

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.403
And then the final
piece, I would say,

00:02:43.403 --> 00:02:46.550
is that we had already made a
lot of investments in BigQuery

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.870
in AI and ML.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:52.440
So combining all these
data sources in one place

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.200
and bringing even things
like Google Workspace

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.750
has proven to be very
effective for us too.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:01.980
And it opened up a lot of
interesting new product ideas

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.005
that we were not thinking of
before, or not possible before.

00:03:05.005 --> 00:03:07.380
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: What are
some of the challenges you're

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.530
solving with machine
learning and data analytics?

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:11.940
AMOL KHER: We are trying to--

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.520
we have three or four
different areas of investment

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.910
in machine learning and data--

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:19.710
data science today.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.720
Part of the area
that we focus on

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.490
is how do we make
our providers--

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:26.820
we internally call it making
a provider superhuman,

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.550
so building tools and
assistive technology that

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:33.270
can help providers become
much more efficient at how

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.300
they do their jobs.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:40.470
As an example, using notetaking,
automatic notetaking--

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:44.910
so because we have the audio
and video conversation that's

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.700
going on, part of the
job after a consult

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.990
is for someone to actually write
down the notes for the patient,

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:56.227
or what if, like, using
NLP and speech-to-text,

00:03:56.227 --> 00:03:58.310
we are able to just take
down those notes for you.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:01.710
So that's one area where we
reduce the amount of burden

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:03.510
on the provider so
that way they can treat

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.020
more patients in some ways.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:08.910
And then another area where
we've tried to help providers

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:14.280
is also how can we make their
case, get them more patients,

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.490
without burdening them too much.

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:21.240
So as an example, depending
on the severity and acuity

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.690
of the patient, how can
we surface those patients

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.840
much quickly to our providers.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.750
And maybe there's others who
are doing already very well.

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.420
They don't need
that much attention.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:34.560
How do you prioritize
that caseload?

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.180
So those are a couple
of applications

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:37.740
that we've seen on
the provider side

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.090
where machine learning has
been very useful in servicing

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:45.528
this information much more
quickly to our providers.

00:04:45.528 --> 00:04:47.570
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: From
our conversation earlier,

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:51.290
I do recall Vidapedia, which
is another major recommendation

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.590
platform that you built
with data from Workspace

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:55.940
and BigQuery.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:59.250
Now, what is it, and
how does it work?

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:00.750
AMOL KHER: Yeah,
well, first of all,

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.680
let me explain what
Vidapedia is, even

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.780
without machine learning.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:07.310
So it's a set of protocols.

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:11.060
It's-- basically, you can think
of them as Google Docs that

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:16.580
have been written that help our
providers and our clinicians

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.010
follow a protocol when
certain things happen.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:21.650
Over time, our
medical organization,

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:24.860
you know, Mike Scahill,
he sort of created

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:29.030
a corpus of almost 150, 200
protocols that help people

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:31.950
know what is the right level--
what is the right thing to do

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:35.940
and what's the right care to
give in a particular situation.

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:38.810
And now, as you can
imagine, no human

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:42.110
is able to store 200 different
sort of protocols in their head

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:42.960
at the same time.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.470
And it becomes a data
access problem, right?

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:49.290
How do you quickly get access
to this sort of information

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:50.570
at high speed?

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:53.960
And so what Vidapedia
Recommendation System does

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.330
on machine learning
is actually we've

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:58.730
kind of indexed all
those documents,

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:01.220
and then we look at
what patients are--

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:04.250
what is the patient provider
interaction that's going on,

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:07.820
and then surface the
right protocol for a given

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:09.040
interaction.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:12.830
So if, during the course
of this chat interaction,

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.230
the machine learning
model detects

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:19.760
that this triggers a
particular protocol,

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:22.670
then it surfaces that to the
provider during the session

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:24.453
or even after the session.

00:06:24.453 --> 00:06:26.120
And so then what
happens is the provider

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.810
is able to give the right
care to the patient.

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:32.420
And so from our point
of view, the provider

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:34.670
becomes more efficient,
at the same time

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:37.010
increasing the level
of quality of the care

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:38.130
that they're providing.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.110
So the team is pretty
excited about that.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.360
And we wouldn't have
been able to do this

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:46.850
if we were not able to
connect what the Google Doc

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:49.730
protocols looked like
from Google Workspace,

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:52.970
take that data, and index
it and store it in BigQuery,

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:55.310
and then be able to
build machine learning

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:56.690
tools on top of that, right?

00:06:56.690 --> 00:07:00.390
And so you can see pretty
quickly, by going to Google,

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:03.170
one of the things we've
been able to nicely connect

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:05.690
is the Google Workspace
data that our providers

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:10.070
use to our actual service,
which is the machine learning

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:14.730
algorithms, and build a
product that's, in my opinion,

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:16.010
something pretty unique.

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:21.530
That-- when we set
out to move to Google,

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:23.130
we had not even
thought about this.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.910
But now that it's there,
it just makes applications

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:28.558
like this actually
possible for us.

00:07:28.558 --> 00:07:30.350
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Yeah, that is so amazing

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:34.370
to hear because it is one of
those unique aspects of using

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:38.970
Google Cloud, because
Workspace data is right there,

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:40.550
and you can just
put it into BigQuery

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:42.980
and start to do stuff with it.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.650
Now, obviously,
this whole workflow

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:47.190
is pretty data intensive.

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:50.000
And this is an
architecture show, so

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:51.830
let's dive into the details.

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:54.470
What does the data
pipeline flow look like?

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:57.320
And what are the data
sources involved?

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:00.890
And how is it making its
way through the tools

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:04.370
to making it useful
in these machine

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.360
learning use cases that
you just talked about?

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.152
AMOL KHER: So we ingest
data from a variety

00:08:08.152 --> 00:08:10.700
of different sources, all
the way at the beginning,

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:15.020
where our health plans might
send us eligibility files,

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:18.950
or they might send us claims
data that tell us a little bit

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:20.270
more about the member.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:24.290
Then the members themselves will
often bring their own devices

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:25.140
into the system.

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:27.530
So for instance, they
might have a Fitbit,

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:29.970
and they might connect
that with the Vida app.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:33.140
So now you're getting to see
the steps that they're taking,

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:35.360
or the weight, or
whatever log-- whatever

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:36.950
they are logging in that.

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:39.950
For diabetes patients, sometimes
we ship out glucometers.

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:44.330
And so then you get to see
their sugar readings and stuff

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:44.940
like that.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:46.730
So all of data is
getting ingested.

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:48.110
Some of it is real-time.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:50.150
Some of it is batched.

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:53.180
And it's-- we're collecting all
of this stuff from different.

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:55.820
Then, even on the provider
side, the providers

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:58.550
are conducting consults,
and they're taking notes

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:00.110
for those consults, right?

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:02.660
So that's another piece
of data that gets ingested

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:04.040
into all of this stuff.

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:06.110
And the goal of
this data platform,

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:10.160
is at the end of the
day, help derive insights

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:12.020
and make decisions on
top of that, right?

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:16.010
And so our goal was to
create a system that's

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:18.440
seamless, where everything
gets stored in one place,

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:21.980
like BigQuery, and
then drive in sets,

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:23.940
build tools on top of that.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:29.870
So it's not just BigQuery data,
which is somewhat unstructured,

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:32.630
but also structured
data and relational data

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:36.500
that we get from
Postgres and MySQL.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:39.050
All of that ultimately
lands in BigQuery

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:42.430
for us, and then applications
get built on top of that,

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:42.930
right?

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:46.490
So our analysts work on
tools, visualization tools,

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:52.130
like Data Studio and Looker,
and our data scientists

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:54.290
build on top of
the BigQuery data

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:57.350
to build machine learning
models and connect that

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:59.910
to our actual services.

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:05.030
One of the things that
we've recently gotten use,

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:08.330
are exploring, is how
can we use Vertex AI

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:11.630
for quickly building these
models and deploying them.

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:14.000
In one of our recent
hackathons, one

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:16.080
of the most impressive
things about this tool

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:19.550
was that even, like, non--

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:21.560
I would say-- machine
learning engineers

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:24.740
are able to quickly
use Vertex AI to try

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:28.260
out and experiment and see
if that might be useful.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:31.970
So I'm pretty excited about
the seamless integration

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:35.270
of the ingest as well as the
analysis engine that we're

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:36.380
building on top of that.

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:37.797
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
That is so cool

00:10:37.797 --> 00:10:40.910
to hear because we just launched
it recently, right, at I/O.

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:45.530
And we have an entire
series of Vertex AI videos

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:47.100
that I've been working on.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:48.510
And we will link those below.

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:51.110
So thanks for the mention
on Vertex AI and how easy

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:55.340
it has made to use the--
to apply machine learning

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:58.790
to any sort of data, and
make the steps easier

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:01.070
and accessible to everybody.

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:04.080
Now, I'm curious about
the transformations.

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:06.420
So you talked about where
you're storing the data.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.450
and a lot about
machine learning.

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:10.730
There must be some sort of
transformations happening.

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:13.130
What are you using for
data transformation?

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:15.080
AMOL KHER: Yeah, today we have--

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:17.330
in the past, we
have been writing

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:20.000
a lot of custom ETL pipelines.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:25.400
We've learned that managing
them, monitoring them, keeping

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:28.280
them stable and running
has been a challenge.

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:31.580
And being a small team,
just like we try and find

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:33.710
ways to do managed
services on Google,

00:11:33.710 --> 00:11:35.600
we've tried to explore
what are other tools

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:36.433
available out there.

00:11:36.433 --> 00:11:40.550
And we've settled on dbt
as the transformation tool

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:43.670
that we plan to currently
use for transforming some

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:46.700
of this data as we move it
from one system to another,

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:50.003
or get it ready for
analysis and stuff.

00:11:50.003 --> 00:11:50.920
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK.

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:52.180
So there's a lot happening.

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:55.330
Obviously, there's data
ingestion, then transformation,

00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:57.820
and then there's machine
learning on top of that.

00:11:57.820 --> 00:12:01.310
How do you manage all of
this stuff in a pipeline?

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:03.160
How do you orchestrate
this pipeline?

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:04.100
AMOL KHER: Well,
I mean, this case,

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:05.267
the answer is pretty simple.

00:12:05.267 --> 00:12:07.600
I think we just use Cloud
Composer for orchestrating

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:10.000
the pipeline,
scheduling all of this,

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:12.920
ingest, processing data, and
running all of that stuff.

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:15.640
So, again, having
these tools ready

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:19.300
and be seamlessly working
well with BigQuery

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:20.840
is beneficial for us.

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:23.290
And I think the team has
sort of taken that up

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:24.822
and implemented them.

00:12:24.822 --> 00:12:26.030
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: All right.

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:28.030
So when it comes to
health care, it's

00:12:28.030 --> 00:12:31.300
important to address the
elephant in the room, obviously

00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:33.400
security, right?

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:36.280
Let's talk a little bit
about high-level security

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:38.830
requirements that
you have to look

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:41.010
for in designing this system.

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:42.010
AMOL KHER: You're right.

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:44.690
I don't actually think of it as
even the elephant in the room.

00:12:44.690 --> 00:12:46.130
It's just, like, always there.

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:48.970
And one of the
reasons that I forgot

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:52.738
to mention when we
initially talked about what

00:12:52.738 --> 00:12:54.280
was the advantage
of going to Google,

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:57.480
is that in the old
system that we had,

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:00.730
we had these two
different Cloud providers

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:02.050
that we were working on.

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:05.110
And one of the fundamental
things with security

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:07.180
is what is your surface area.

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:10.690
And we've had a lot of data
flowing between one system

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:11.590
to the other.

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:17.260
That creates-- that opens us up
for higher surface area, right?

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:19.120
Someone can--
something can go wrong

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:21.440
and people can get access to it.

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:27.530
So because the patient data that
we work on is fairly sensitive,

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:30.130
we have to be extremely
careful of protecting it.

00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:33.310
The privacy laws around
this are pretty stringent.

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:37.780
So actually moving to Google has
been beneficial in that sense,

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:41.620
because, first of all, we are
a small company, as a startup,

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:45.340
and so I always tell
everyone I'd rather just

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:46.960
use Google as my shield.

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:52.570
We have thousands of engineers
at Google who are thinking--

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:55.180
living and breathing, constantly
thinking about security

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:57.590
and how to secure the
Google Cloud environment.

00:13:57.590 --> 00:14:00.370
And so just being part
of that whole ecosystem

00:14:00.370 --> 00:14:02.740
helps us immensely,
where we don't

00:14:02.740 --> 00:14:06.250
have to worry about
our own security thing

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:09.310
as long as we follow the best
practices that Google lays out

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:10.370
for us.

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:14.980
And I think we are also,
sort of, combining that

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:17.080
with the Google
Workspace side of stuff,

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:20.740
where you can enable things
like data loss protection.

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:23.140
We can mask PII data.

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:26.620
All of these things just become
instantly available to us,

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:31.000
and we don't have to spend
a lot of our mental capacity

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.580
to think and evaluate
what we need to do.

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:36.070
It also helps us with
our customer site

00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:38.320
because people are
more likely to believe

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:39.950
when we tell them,
like, hey, we've

00:14:39.950 --> 00:14:44.500
used and turned on whatever
Google is doing in this space.

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:47.620
Also with our health
plans, for instance,

00:14:47.620 --> 00:14:51.213
they are often looking
for a vote of confidence.

00:14:51.213 --> 00:14:52.630
They are kind of
saying, hey, what

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:54.640
Cloud vendor are you using?

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:56.710
And when you tell them
that you're on Google,

00:14:56.710 --> 00:14:59.080
they're often pretty
satisfied with the fact that--

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:03.220
if it's a big company like
that is sort of taking care

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:05.410
of the security, we kind of--

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:09.088
I call it bump drafting behind
a big company like that.

00:15:09.088 --> 00:15:10.630
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
No, it's extremely

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:12.730
helpful because a
lot of the times,

00:15:12.730 --> 00:15:14.890
we think that going
with multicloud

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:18.010
or having things in
hybrid cloud is good.

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:20.120
But then, especially
in this case,

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:24.010
when it comes to security, it's
a little bit like you said.

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:26.140
Reducing your surface
area of attack

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:28.420
is the most important
thing to think about.

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:31.150
Now, we talked a lot about
the data analytics and machine

00:15:31.150 --> 00:15:32.830
learning architecture.

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:35.320
I want to pivot a
tiny bit and learn

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:38.450
more about the application
architecture itself.

00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:41.530
Now, how is the web app set
up, the compute, the database?

00:15:41.530 --> 00:15:44.750
I assume it's all
normal, standard stuff.

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:46.870
But I would love to
hear about that as well.

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:49.690
AMOL KHER: Because we did this
migration fairly recently--

00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:52.950
we moved from a different
cloud vendor to Google--

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:55.750
we were able to work with
a Google partner, SADA

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:59.830
and really use some of the
best practices that there are--

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:02.950
and modern practices that
are available to us, right?

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:05.560
And so it's not very surprising.

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:10.120
It's like a standard, I would
say, a three-tier architecture.

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:13.990
You have load balancers at
the front, just deployed in

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:17.410
and Kubernetes Engine, Google
Kubernetes Engine, and then

00:16:17.410 --> 00:16:20.320
a middle, sort
of, API layer that

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:23.980
returns a whole bunch
of requests response.

00:16:23.980 --> 00:16:26.470
And at the end you, have
a Postgres database.

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:29.350
And so architecturally,
it's not that complex.

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:30.110
Simple is good.

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:32.840
It's easy to scale,
especially on Google.

00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:35.860
So, for me, that's
been the great thing.

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:39.730
Even the database is like
moved over to not-- like,

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:41.425
our own custom sort
of Postgres thing.

00:16:41.425 --> 00:16:43.150
We're on GCS Postgres.

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:45.610
And that way,
scaling of database,

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:49.840
which used to be a big
discussion point and debate,

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:52.850
it just becomes a few
clicks of a button,

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:55.658
and you can now vertically
scale a database if you want.

00:16:55.658 --> 00:16:57.950
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So, well,
you mentioned a little bit

00:16:57.950 --> 00:16:59.000
about migration.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:01.110
How did you go about doing that?

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:04.760
AMOL KHER: So I work with
the Google support team

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:06.500
that I have--

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:08.390
who I work with pretty closely.

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:11.300
And we kind of knew that--

00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:15.230
I had made up my mind that we
wanted to consolidate our cloud

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:16.400
system into one place.

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:18.089
And Google was a choice.

00:17:18.089 --> 00:17:20.480
And so with Will,
we kind of evaluated

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:24.770
a few different partners that
could help us do the migration,

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:27.685
ended up choosing SADA for
a few different reasons.

00:17:27.685 --> 00:17:29.810
I think they are pretty
well known in the industry.

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:33.410
Part of the major
migration plan here

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:37.160
was around how do we
transfer all of the data that

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:39.470
was in the other
cloud system, have

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:42.980
minimum downtime
for our customers,

00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:45.770
and then also how do
we make sure we set up

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:50.600
this new system in a much more
modern and easy to deploy way

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:53.785
so that our engineers,
going forward,

00:17:53.785 --> 00:17:55.160
can take ownership
of that system

00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:58.910
and be able to continuously
deploy the system.

00:17:58.910 --> 00:18:02.090
We probably work for about a
quarter, quarter and a half

00:18:02.090 --> 00:18:03.800
to come up with the entire plan.

00:18:03.800 --> 00:18:06.140
And when they
wrote some of the--

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:09.410
they helped us write on the
code around terraform scripts

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:12.230
that can help us
deploy to Google.

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:14.420
And then on the actual
day of migration,

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:18.500
they also are on-hand to
make sure we're orchestrating

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:22.370
the most, sort of, together, so
shutting down the whole system,

00:18:22.370 --> 00:18:26.430
migrating data, and
turning on the new system.

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:28.100
So it was a pretty
exciting time.

00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:31.620
But I'm so glad that it
went without a hitch.

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:34.577
And now we're pretty
happy with this system.

00:18:34.577 --> 00:18:36.410
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: It
sounds so cool to hear

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:39.313
the back story of
the migration, right,

00:18:39.313 --> 00:18:41.480
because we just talked about
the architecture, which

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:42.740
is the current architecture.

00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:44.690
And then-- and
then when you hear

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:47.480
the migration story and
all that goes into it,

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:50.330
and teams like SADA and
the Google Cloud team,

00:18:50.330 --> 00:18:52.520
and your team combined
together, like,

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:56.390
bring the whole thing to life.

00:18:56.390 --> 00:18:58.190
This is pretty amazing to hear.

00:18:58.190 --> 00:18:59.850
Now what's next for you guys?

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:03.890
How are you planning to augment
this existing architecture?

00:19:03.890 --> 00:19:06.650
And what else is
underway for Vida?

00:19:06.650 --> 00:19:09.230
AMOL KHER: Actually,
now the fun part

00:19:09.230 --> 00:19:12.600
starts, from my point of view,
because it's all in one place.

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:17.840
And my call for the
team is now let's

00:19:17.840 --> 00:19:21.320
double down even more, make more
use of more managed services

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:22.550
that Google offers.

00:19:22.550 --> 00:19:25.220
And in particular, I'm
very excited about a couple

00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:26.930
of areas, which is
the Google Health Care

00:19:26.930 --> 00:19:29.720
API and the FHIR-related
capabilities

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:32.120
that Google is
invested in, right?

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:35.860
So historically, FHIR
has not been a very--

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:37.670
it has not had a
lot of traction.

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:40.580
It's been increasing
little by little.

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:42.980
But we've heard over the
last year, year and a half,

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:45.440
things have moved quite
a bit in that space.

00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:48.680
And just like we are
storing all this information

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:53.450
about medical data in one-- in
our own proprietary formats,

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:57.470
we want to now start using
Google's FHIR storage

00:19:57.470 --> 00:19:59.130
as well to start
storing the data

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:02.300
so that it's easier to exchange
this sort of information

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:05.270
with other networks
outside of Vida.

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:09.650
So the classic example is,
like, you go to a doctor.

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:13.160
They refer you to another
doctor for some other reason.

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:17.390
You would expect that your
data gets securely and clearly

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:20.450
transferred over from one
place so that the other doctor

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:22.760
doesn't have to ask you
the same thing again,

00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:25.610
or trying to understand
what you are trying to--

00:20:25.610 --> 00:20:27.320
what are they trying to treat.

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:30.020
And so the same thing,
same challenges,

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:31.890
are coming for us as well.

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:35.090
So if we are treating
someone with a condition

00:20:35.090 --> 00:20:36.800
that we are not--

00:20:36.800 --> 00:20:41.030
that we currently don't support,
and we want to refer them out

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:44.360
to someone else, we want to be
able to create a system where

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:47.060
our data just gets very
seamlessly transferred over

00:20:47.060 --> 00:20:51.570
to another doctor, like maybe
an inpatient experience as well.

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:53.450
So this is what I'm
pretty excited about,

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:57.080
because Google Health Care
APIs and the FHIR capabilities

00:20:57.080 --> 00:21:00.560
that Google is
building on are just

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:03.290
things like where applications
can be built on top of.

00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:06.320
And I don't have to worry
about the protocol layer,

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:09.625
and how is it stored,
and all that fun stuff.

00:21:09.625 --> 00:21:10.625
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:21:10.625 --> 00:21:13.880
Just store the data, let
the API do the thing,

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:16.400
and just access the
API to do the thing.

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:17.067
AMOL KHER: Yeah.

00:21:17.067 --> 00:21:18.650
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, thanks, Amol.

00:21:18.650 --> 00:21:20.280
It was awesome to
have you on the show.

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:22.580
AMOL KHER: Well, thanks again
for inviting me as well.

00:21:22.580 --> 00:21:25.610
And it was great to
showcase how we've

00:21:25.610 --> 00:21:26.970
built Vida on top of Google.

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:28.970
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well,
we learned a lot today,

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:31.790
how to set up a data pipeline
for health care systems,

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:34.850
and, the most amazing part,
how beautifully the Google

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:38.240
Workspace data can be combined
with BigQuery to create

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:39.800
a recommendations engine.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:41.780
Well, if you are interested
in learning more,

00:21:41.780 --> 00:21:44.540
there's an entire blog
linked below that dives

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:47.450
into the details of how
Vida Health and the team

00:21:47.450 --> 00:21:49.430
at our implementation
partner, SADA,

00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:51.650
brought this
architecture to life.

00:21:51.650 --> 00:21:54.220
Want to see more real life
architecture like this?

